        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:56:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63683,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Hormonal or irritative stimuli are the main factors inducing endometrial metaplasia, with a mutational origin in some cases {21126963}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:56:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63684,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Endometrial metaplasia occurs secondary to altered hormonal levels, repair, endometrial breakdown, chronic inflammation, or polyp infarction {7212146; 21126963; 29257038}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:56:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63685,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:56:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63686,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 232,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Eosinophilic (oncocytic) metaplasia has uniform central nuclei and abundant, densely eosinophilic or granular cytoplasm (with numerous mitochondria) {9891237}. Ciliated (tubal) metaplasia shows numerous apical cilia {21126963}. In hobnail metaplasia, single-layered, mitotically inactive cuboidal cells project over the surface {29257038}. Mucinous metaplasia has columnar cells with mucin-rich cytoplasm and may be seen in polyps and in papillary proliferation {6885226; 25485781; 21126963}. Squamous metaplasia may show mature squamous cells with keratinization and/or glycogenation, or it may show morules, which are nests of immature spindle cells with indistinct cell borders and bland nuclei. The immunoprofile (&beta;-catenin+, CDX2+, CD10+, p16+, EMA&minus;, ER&minus;, p63&minus;) is the opposite of that of mature squamous metaplasia {16857799; 18752499; 7195657}. Clear cell metaplasia shows abundant clear, glycogen-rich cytoplasm or lipid-rich foamy cytoplasm {11756756}. Papillary proliferation shows papillae with fibrovascular stromal cores covered by cytologically bland epithelium, and it commonly occurs with mucinous or other metaplasias. Simple papillae have short and non-branching stalks; complex papillae have elongated stalks and complex branches {23211295; 11684950; 29445890}. Some of these alterations are associated with metaplasia, and each may be associated with precursor lesions and/or carcinoma; therefore, the context of identification of these metaplastic processes is important. Syncytial papillary change (papillary syncytial metaplasia) is reparative rather than a true metaplasia. Eosinophilic cells form syncytial aggregates, buds, or stroma-free papillae, with prominent neutrophils {8322711; 18753967; 21323964}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T16:56:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
